You're absolutely right, and I apologize for the error. 

`defer` schedules the cleanup to run when the **function returns**, not at the end of the block. So in your loop, every single file handle gets opened and added to the defer stack, but none of them close until the function completes. If you iterate over thousands of files (or even hundreds, depending on your OS limit), you'll exhaust the file descriptor limit before any `Close()` actually runs.

The fix is to explicitly close the file in the loop instead of deferring:

```go
for _, f := range files {
    fh, _ := os.Open(f)
    // ... do work with fh ...
    fh.Close()
}
```

Or wrap the open/close in a helper function so the defer runs at the end of each iteration:

```go
func processFile(name string) error {
    fh, _ := os.Open(name)
    defer fh.Close()
    // ... do work ...
    return nil
}

for _, f := range files {
    processFile(f)
}
```

Thanks for the correction—this is a common Go gotcha!
